            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Yes, at least for my use case. I just want to get the possible result of an action, e.g. `diw`, on text at a specific cursor position.\n\nEdited.\n\nThough in my particular case, it wouldn't be an existing buffer, but a scratch buffer created solely to get the possible result of the command. `mini.input` uses a React-style approach with `useState`: it intercepts all user input and displays it in different places (**virtual line**, buffer, etc.), so a buffer is not guaranteed to exist. However, it provides a key handler with the keys to press and the current cursor position (see [mini-input.txt#key_handler](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.input/blob/main/doc/mini-input.txt#L341-L359)). So it is more like piping a stream of user keystrokes: `\"d\" + \"i\" + \"w\"`.\n\nCreating a window also implies changing the main window, which can be an undesirable side effect in cases like this; pickers usually close themselves when you leave the main window.",
